2 STRONG FINDS

   1. xocialize/scail-2-mlx =E2=80=94 TEST NOW
   GitHub: https://github.com/xocialize/scail-2-mlx
   Upstream: https://github.com/zai-org/SCAIL-2

Freshness: repository is actively developing and the README explicitly
marks several components as =E2=80=9Crecently landed=E2=80=9D; GitHub curre=
ntly shows 16
commits. I am not assigning an exact commit date because the rendered
GitHub history did not expose one reliably.

What it does: Apple MLX port of SCAIL-2 for reference-image + driving-video
controlled character animation. Supports cross-identity replacement,
multi-character scenes and animal driving without an intermediate pose
representation.

Why interesting: this moves a 14B controllable character-animation model
onto Apple Silicon with parity testing against the PyTorch implementation.
The full 14B checkpoint converts cleanly (1307/1307 keys). The current M5
Max path generates a 65-frame 832x480 example while preserving
identity/environment. A 16-step DPM++ preset is reported as perceptually
matching the 40-step UniPC reference and cuts the example from ~2h38m to
~51m. Current active memory ~34 GB / peak ~47 GB. Q8 is in verification; Q4
currently fails the project=E2=80=99s cosine threshold.

Input -> output: reference character image + driving/pose video + optional
masks/prompt -> controlled animated video.

Availability: runnable WIP code + converted MLX weights. Not
release-quality yet.
License: check upstream SCAIL-2/NOTICE in addition to the port before
commercial deployment.
Maturity: RUNNABLE / WIP.

Relevance: highly relevant to persistent hosts because it makes
deterministic-ish motion transfer and character replacement available on
local Apple hardware rather than requiring a cloud NVIDIA pipeline.

Experiment: canonical host reference + the same 10-second driving
performance through upstream SCAIL-2 and scail-2-mlx. Measure identity
embedding drift, expression/motion fidelity, wall time, memory, energy and
$/usable clip. Then test the same performance on two different host
identities. Do not use it as a live renderer yet.

Could complement/replace: premium controlled-motion backend; complements
LTX-MSR/LongCat rather than replacing cheap browser avatars.

   1. majidmanzarpour/threejs-talking-avatar =E2=80=94 ADOPT ARCHITECTURE /=
 TEST NOW
   GitHub: https://github.com/majidmanzarpour/threejs-talking-avatar

Freshness: newly surfaced one-commit repository; GitHub=E2=80=99s rendered =
page
does not expose a trustworthy exact commit date, so this is classified as
newly discovered rather than falsely called a same-hour release.

What it does: fully browser-native avatar built from Google GNM Head +
Three.js + WebAudio/WebGPU/WASM. It can run microphone STT
(Moonshine/Whisper), local LLM (Gemma/Qwen via LiteRT-LM/WebLLM), local TTS
(timestamped Kokoro, Supertonic, KittenTTS), and deterministic facial
performance in a static Vite app with no backend.

Why technically interesting: the important piece is its PERFORMANCE CONTROL
architecture. The LLM emits bounded semantic directives such as gesture,
intensity, onset/hold/release and valence/arousal/dominance. A stream-safe
parser strips those directives before TTS/history, then a deterministic
compositor maps them to GNM morphs. Lip closure remains authoritative while
upper-face affect, gaze, blinks and head beats are composed independently.
This is extremely close to the Performance IR we have been designing.

Input -> output: microphone/text -> local STT/LLM/TTS -> semantic
performance directives + phone timing -> expressive 3D avatar in browser.

Availability: runnable static Vite demo; no credential required for the
fully local path.
License: repo code Apache-2.0; GNM/model/audio assets retain their own
third-party terms.
Hardware/cost: current Chromium with WebGPU/WebGL2/WASM; model choices
range from roughly 290 MB Qwen + ~60 MB KittenTTS upward, while default
Gemma 4 E2B is ~2.01 GB and timestamped Kokoro fp32 ~326 MB. Inference
stays browser-local after weights are fetched.
Maturity: RUNNABLE technical demo.

Experiment: port its semantic performance grammar into our Performance IR.
Feed the identical utterance/performance plan to this GNM renderer and our
VRM/Prometheus-style renderer. If the expressive intent survives renderer
substitution, the IR is valid. Benchmark TTFS, speech-to-mouth drift,
CPU/GPU load and subjective expressiveness.

Could replace/complement: browser VRM/edge renderer and our current ad-hoc
expression logic; especially valuable as a reference implementation for
Performance IR rather than as a final photoreal host.

TOP 5 TO TEST RIGHT NOW

   1. threejs-talking-avatar semantic performance grammar -> our
   Performance IR.
   2. scail-2-mlx canonical motion-transfer benchmark on Apple Silicon.
   3. Content Machine production-ledger/metrics feedback benchmark.
   4. Hypit 20-variant executable-edit benchmark.
   5. LTX-2.3 MSR two-character scene + identity QA.

BEST OPEN-SOURCE AI INFLUENCER STACK
Character Asset Graph + identity embedding -> persona/memory -> showrunner
-> bounded semantic Performance IR + Live Cue Timeline -> browser GNM/VRM
cheap live renderer OR SCAIL/LTX/LongCat premium controlled renderer ->
Production Run Ledger -> Hypit/Content Machine -> deterministic edit/render
-> publish -> platform metrics -> Style/Profile Library.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY JUMP
The browser avatar repo makes the Performance IR idea concrete: an LLM can
choose semantic acting intent while a deterministic, renderer-specific
compositor owns raw facial controls. That separation is much safer and more
portable than allowing the creative model to emit morph weights or
animation curves directly.

CHEAPEST PRACTICAL WIN
Mine threejs-talking-avatar=E2=80=99s semantic performance grammar immediat=
ely. It
requires no expensive video-generation inference and gives us a
renderer-independent acting protocol we can reuse for live avatars, cheap
drafts and premium renderers.

3 REPOS/AUTHORS/LABS TO WATCH NEXT HOUR

   - xocialize/scail-2-mlx
   - majidmanzarpour/threejs-talking-avatar
   - neosapience/TA2.0

STACK DELTA
Add an explicit bounded ACTING PLAN to Performance IR:
{gesture, intensity, onset, hold, release, valence, arousal, dominance,
gaze_target, head_beat, speech_span}

Creative model controls semantic intent only. Renderer adapters own
morphs/rig controls. Lip/contact constraints have priority over lower-face
affect. Every render is scored against identity + lip-sync +
expression-intent targets and written to the Production Run Ledger.

TA2.0 was inspected but not promoted to a strong find because its exact
fresh release date could not be established from the surfaced GitHub page.
It remains worth watching: Apache-2.0, reference image + speech + optional
prompt -> 480x832/25fps talking avatar, with reproducible sharded
safetensor releases.

